January 20th's editorial, "Right Pleads for Bias Free Voice," stated, "It is
not only _University Chronicle_, but all of media who some denounce as being
too far to the left.  Perhaps this is because journalists as a whole are better
informed and less likely to believe everything they are told."

How absurd.  Leftists are _more_ apt to believe Establishment "truths" than are
rightists.  The proper meaning of the term "liberal," one who is sincerely 
tolerant of new ideas and other's opinions, seldom can be rightly applied to
most leftists.

Today's journalists purport to offer true diversity of ideas and opinion, but
as is typical in today's "style before substance" society, this is popular
myth.

Through clever selectivity of topical perspectives in both general news and
editorial material, the Establishment media guarantee a _predetermined_ spec-
trum of thought, from politically moderate to ultra-leftist, will be conveyed tthe public.  Rarely are right-of-center views, especially those of the so-
called "extreme Right," given a fair and equal hearing.  The few pieces allowed
through the "News Filter" to provide an illusory "balance" are usually irrele-
vant, inaccurate, or inane.

It is through this politically correct spectrum of ideas and opinion the media
successfully manipulate the American People, and indirectly, our institutions.

The maxim "all the news that's fit to print" has been perverted by today's
media into a policy of "all the news that fits our agenda."

America's media can be truly free only when dedication to facts and genuine 
intellectual diversity, not one's own political desires, is the guiding spirit
of every journalist.

-- Milton John Kleim, Jr.

Taken from the Saint Cloud State _University Chronicle_, Tuesday, January 24th.



