In terms of a slow, progressive trend -- no, there is no evidence for it. But 
that's not what I'm basing my assertion. Indeed, about the surest way to make a 
fool of yourself in predicting the future is to simply extend current trends 
forward. History works in reverses and spurts, not continual progress from a to 
b to c.

Here is my prediction/thesis:That sometime in the 1 to 5 years, a major 
University will find itself the target of a civil rights (NOT First Amendment) 
lawsuit based on "offensive" or "degrading" material found on the Internet. Since 
it receives federal funds, it will be obliged to obey all Federal statutes 
regarding equal protection. (Note that this is why the ACLU can claim that 
prayers before college football games violate seperation of Church and State)

Attempts to ban this or that newsgroup in order to placate the hordes of the 
easily offended will fail, in large part because newsgroups are more-or-less 
meaingless -- that is, no force on Earth could stop me from posting any offensive 
message to any unmoderated group. Sure, I'd get flamed to death, but that's why 
God gave us anon servers, or, even better, "10 free hours on America Online" 
(Prodigy, CI$, Delphi...). (Do you really think all those people posting 
braindead messages from commercial services are just newbies? Most of them are 
posers)

The first lawsuit to achieve publicity will generate scores of others, all filed 
long before the first is close to being resolved. In general, college 
presidents could give even the French lessons in the art of abject 
surrender;rather than fight lawsuits, they will simply impose draconian 
restrictions on Internet and Usenet access. The news admins and techie types will 
object, but no one fears a riot by Computer Science majors, except maybe at MIT. 
But incoming Freshmen will expect Internet;existing students will be addicted to 
it.

Welcome the hordes of local commercial providers. Since the students will be 
paying for it themselves, the college is utterly unaccountable. The college will 
do all it can to encourage students to aquire accounts, since a wired campus 
populace is generally beneficial. The transition from "every students gets an 
account from the college" to "every student orders an account at orientation when 
they get their phone and fridge" will probably take about eight years, total, to 
complete. (Random guess based on no studies whatsoever)

